snippet.textDisplay:
@Tammy-g1v5t And to clarify, my point about VPNs has nothing to do with politics. It’s simply about a basic, concrete fact: in mainland China you cannot access YouTube, which has been blocked since 2009. As a result, a Chinese citizen who watches YouTube is doing so illegally, because it requires the use of a VPN.

There are only a few exceptions. Access is not restricted in Hong Kong and Macao, and it may also be possible through roaming with a non-Chinese SIM card. Outside of those cases, using an unauthorized VPN in mainland China carries legal consequences, even if enforcement can be selective.

So this isn’t about ideology or hidden narratives. It’s about the clear difference between a platform being accessible but imperfectly covered by the media, and a platform being outright banned by law.

